We propose FutrTrack, a multi-modal tracker that harnesses a holistic camera-LiDAR fusion approach to provide rich attention queries for track association. FutrTrack makes use of existing object detectors, refines them using a smoother model, and then tracks them using our new multi-modal querybased tracker that relies on multi-modal features extracted from both camera and LiDAR sensors. The query-based tracker. Benchmark validation shows improved average Multi Object Tracking Precision (aMOTP) with smoother trajectories and reduced identity switches on the nuScenes (Caesar et al., 2020) and KITTI (Geiger et al., 2012) datasets. Our main contributions in this paper are as follows:
• A multimodal transformer-based tracking framework that extends query-based tracking to jointly reason over camera and LiDAR modalities, enabling robust multi-object tracking in fused 3D space.
• A camera–LiDAR projection and fusion module that aligns image features with LiDAR point representations to construct a unified bird’s-eye-view (BEV) embedding optimized for tracking rather than detection.
• On nuScenes and KITTI, our method outperforms prior transformer-based trackers, particularly in occlusion scenarios and for small objects.


2 RELATED WORK
MOT has seen significant developments in recent years, particularly with the incorporation of deep learning methods (Hasan et al., 2022a; Hasan et al., 2022b; Erabati and Araujo, 2023). Current MOT research generally falls into one of the following paradigms: Tracking-by-Detection (Wojke et al., 2017; Bewley et al., 2016; Wang et al., 2024), Joint Detection and Tracking (Meinhardt et al., 2022; Bergmann et al., 2019), and Tracking-byAttention (Zeng et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2022; Ding et al., 2023). Methods in the Joint Detection and Tracking and Tracking-by-Attention categories largely depend on rich object feature extraction, which demands substantial GPU computation and data. Such requirements are difficult to meet with the limited processing capacity of autonomous vehicles, especially in online modes. In addition, these approaches often do not outperform the simpler Tracking-by-Detection strategy in practice. For these reasons, this work focuses on a lightweight transformer Tracking-by-Attention method. Traditional MOT pipelines separate object detection and tracking, i.e. detection first, then data association like in SORT (Bewley et al., 2016), DeepSORT (Wojke et al., 2017) and extended Kalman filtering (EKF). With transformer-based detectors like DETR (Carion et al., 2020), end-to-end tracking approaches emerged, using track queries across frames to fuse detection with temporal continuity (Meinhardt et al., 2022; Zeng et al., 2022; Zhang et al., 2023a). MOTR (Zeng et al., 2022) adapts that paradigm for image-based queries, embedding spatial queries, and enabling joint detection and tracking directly from camera data. Baseline methods such as CenterPoint-Track (Yin et al., 2021; Pang et al., 2022), perform data associations using Kalman Filter, Extended Kalman Filter, and other probabilistic models. Prior fusion tracking works mostly follow detection-first pipelines, but few integrate fusion features within end-to-end MOT frameworks. Recently, a DETR-based MOT, LiDAR MOT-DETR (Teye et al., 2025) exploits point-based or voxel-based features to localize and track objects in 3D. This method is particularly good at refining bounding boxes, but is limited to only considering the LiDAR-based feature when matching objects to tracks.
Multi-Modal (LiDAR–Camera) Fusion for Detection and Tracking: Fusion methods range from early fusion (raw-level concatenation), through feature-level fusion (e.g., PointPainting (Vora et al.,
2020), PointAugmenting (Li et al., 2020)), to BEVlevel fusion (e.g. BEVFusion (Liu et al., 2023), TransFusion (Bai et al., 2022)). BEVFusion explicitly projects camera and LiDAR features into BEV, enabling transformer-based or convolutional backbones to reason jointly. These have proven to work well in object detection tasks, and FutrTrack exploits these applications in query-based tracking.
Appearance for Re-identification: Camera-based tracking benefits significantly from appearance features. ReID modules (e.g., in DeepSORT (Wojke et al., 2017)) use image embedding distances to enforce identity consistency. Introducing appearance cues into LiDAR-based or fusion-based trackers remains under-explored, especially in unified DETRstyle models. This gap is what our work seeks to bridge and serve as a baseline to camera-LiDAR fusion tracking using transformers. Our method sits at the intersection of DETR-based MOT, BEV-level multimodal fusion, and appearanceaware tracking. FutrTrack is, to the best of our knowledge, the first framework to integrate all three.
3 METHOD
FutrTrack is a query-based transformer multi-object tracker inspired by LiDAR MOT-DETR (Teye et al., 2025) by introducing a camera-LiDAR fusion step and extending query-based tracking to work with these fused features. The overall architecture and components are described in figure 2. With two main components, smoother m and fusion tracker Φ, FutrTrack refines the predictions of the bounding boxes and tracks them using a query-based fusion attention mechanism. FutrTrack works by selecting bounding boxes with centers x, y, z, size w, l, h, yaw angle θ and confidence c from an object detector. A set of these bounding boxes B = {x, y, z, w, l, h, θ, c}, across a temporal window τ are refined using a similar smoothing mechanism m as LiDAR MOT-DETR.
